<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orhan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1281–<a href="/years/1362">1362</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Orhan was the second ruler of the Ottoman state, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-i">Osman I</a>. He captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a> in 1326 and made it the dynasty's first proper capital. His reign marked the transition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> from a raiding frontier confederation into a territorial state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orhan.jpg" alt="Orhan" width="251" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Metehanzade_orhangazi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Orhan was born in Söğüt, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-i">Osman I</a> and his consort Malhun Hatun. Osman died in 1323 or 1324, and Orhan succeeded him. According to Ottoman tradition he proposed to his brother Alaeddin that they share the emerging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>, but Alaeddin refused, since their father had designated Orhan sole successor, and accepted only the revenues of a single village near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a>. Most writers credit Alaeddin with laws on dress and with the creation of a paid standing army, the infantry corps called the Yaya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Orhan conquered most of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, much of it Byzantine. At Pelekanon in June 1329 his disciplined troops routed the army of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-iii-palaiologos">Andronikos III Palaiologos</a>, who never again fought an Ottoman field battle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> surrendered in 1331 after a siege of three years, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> fell in 1337, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Scutari</a> in 1338. In 1345 he took the Turkish principality of Karesi after its ruler's death left two rival sons, which brought his lands to the Dardanelles. He also took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> from the Ahis.</p><p>In the Byzantine civil war of 1341 to 1347, the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-kantakouzenos">John VI Kantakouzenos</a> gave his daughter Theodora in marriage to Orhan and used Ottoman warriors, allowing them to loot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. Around 1352 Kantakouzenos granted the use of the fortress of Çimpe, and after an earthquake devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a>, Orhan's son Süleyman Pasha occupied the town, giving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> a foothold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Orhan is also credited with the formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a>, a corps of a thousand boys chosen from conquered Christian families. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> ranked him first among the Turcoman kings in wealth, lands and military forces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his last years Orhan left most of the powers of state to his second son Murad and lived in seclusion at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a>. In 1356 a Genoese captain kidnapped his young son Halil, and John V Palaiologos ransomed him for 100,000 hyperpyra. In 1357 his eldest son and likely heir, Süleyman Pasha, died after a fall from his horse near Bolayır. Orhan died soon after, in March 1362 at Bursa, and was buried in the tomb called Gümüşlü Kumbet, with his wife and children. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a> succeeded him and had Halil executed, and under Orhan the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> had moved from a frontier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Orhan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Orhan</b> captured Bursa in 1326 and made it the dynasty’s first true capital, and a raiding confederation began to become a territorial state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>An Ottoman army led by <b>Orhan</b> I defeated a Byzantine expeditionary force under Andronikos III at the Battle of Pelekanon.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-10">10 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Orhan</b> annexed the beylik, a process usually dated to around 1345, though resistance continued for some years.</blockquote><a href="/state/karasids">Karasids</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Söğüt, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Bursa, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.02,30.18&amp;year=1333" aria-label="Söğüt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5293 732 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Orhan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5743 1002h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5743 1002h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.02,30.18&amp;year=1333">What was here in 1333? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
